 "case:briefing.0": "Bristol. Tuesday 10 March 2026. On the top floor of No. 31 Queen Square, a Georgian house on the north side of the square, are the offices of Nightjar: twenty-two people and a sleep and meditation app with a million listeners. Last Friday, 6 March, Nightjar was to be sold to Halcyon Health of San Francisco for £60 million.",
 "case:briefing.1": "At ten past seven that Friday, the cleaner found Will Harding, 43, Nightjar’s co-founder and chief executive, dead at his desk. He had been struck on the head with the glass award from his shelf. His sports watch stopped finding his pulse at 21:34 the night before, Thursday 5 March.",
 "case:briefing.2": "The police have arrested Joel Lacey, 29, Nightjar’s lead developer. At ten past six on Thursday the whole office heard him shout at Will: ‘If you sign it tomorrow, I’ll tell them myself!’ He left at 20:51 and ran home, as he does every Thursday. His running app says ten kilometres in fifty minutes, but the run took sixty-five: from 21:24 to 21:38 he sat on a bench in Queen Square, twenty metres from No. 31’s bike-store door. He’s on bail, and the police expect to charge him with murder.",
 "case:briefing.3": "Will’s widow, Claire Harding, has hired you. ‘At twenty past nine Will messaged me: “Joel’s right. I’m not signing it.” Fourteen minutes later he was dead. Why would Joel kill the man who’d just agreed with him?’",
 "case:briefing.4": "Fiona Brodie, Will’s co-founder, is running Nightjar now, and has given you the run of the office. Everyone who was there on Thursday is still in Bristol.",
 "case:epilogue.0": "Fiona Brodie repeated her confession at the police station on the Wednesday, with her solicitor beside her, and that evening she was charged with Will’s murder. Joel’s bail was cancelled the same night. On the Friday the police told him, in writing, that he would not be charged.",
 "case:epilogue.1": "The kitchen behind her on the call was a photograph she had taken one sunny afternoon the May before. She had used it on every call for a year, and the lawyers in New York had never once wondered about the weather in Bristol. She took Joel’s pages from Will’s desk and shredded them at home; she never knew there were two. When the police checked her phone, it put her in Queen Square all evening.",
 "case:epilogue.2": "Halcyon withdrew on the Monday. That night, for the first time since September, nobody listened to ‘Rain on a Tin Roof’ at three in the morning: Growth Partners had switched off all 412,316 of its phones. Kai told the police everything he knew and lost his job. Larchwood wrote its eight million down to almost nothing, and Gavin went back to London on the first train.",
 "case:epilogue.3": "Nightjar didn’t die. Claire kept Will’s shares, the twenty-two of them voted to carry on, and in June a smaller buyer paid a fairer price for 631,000 real listeners. Joel is the chief technology officer now. On the screen by his desk there’s a graph of the night, and at three in the morning it’s as low and ragged as the rest.",
 "case:epilogue.4": "In April Joel ran London with WILL on the back of his vest, and finished in 2:58:41. At the line, out of habit, he looked at his watch. Moving time 2:58:41. Elapsed time 2:58:41. He hadn’t stopped once.",
 "case:hints.h_accuse.levels.0": "Joel never went in. Who was already upstairs?",
