 "case:timeline.events.tl_run.time": "20:56",
 "case:timeline.prompt": "Put the events in the order they really happened. Times you know for certain are shown; reason out the rest.",
 "case:timeline.title": "Thursday evening, reconstructed",
 "case:title": "Moving Time",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_fiona_alive.insight": "Will’s heart stopped at 21:34. His light, on a switch by the door, went off at 21:36. Somebody switched it off after he died, and the only person on the floor was Fiona.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_fiona_alive.response.0": "…He was dead. When I switched it off, he was already dead.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_fiona_alive.response.1": "Gavin’s message came at nine minutes past nine: Will wasn’t signing, the numbers were wrong. I sat in the Nest smiling at three lawyers for twenty minutes. Then I told them my Wi-Fi was dying, and I walked down the floor to Will.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_fiona_alive.response.2": "He had Joel’s pages on his desk, and the Growth Partners contract with my name on it. He said he’d tell Halcyon at nine, and then the police. I begged him. Seven years. My house. And he said, ‘Go home, Fi.’ Just like that. As if I were a child.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_fiona_alive.response.3": "The award was on the shelf by the door. I don’t remember picking it up. I remember standing there afterwards, and taking the pages, and switching his light off so the building would look empty. Then I went back to the Nest, said ‘Sorry! Router’, and smiled for another hour.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_fiona_alive.title": "A dead man’s light",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_fiona_home.insight": "At 21:02 and at 21:51 the sun was shining across Fiona’s kitchen, and her clock said ten to three. It had been dark since six, and raining. Her kitchen was a photograph.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_fiona_home.response.0": "…It’s a photo. My kitchen, last May. I use it on every call. My real kitchen is two teenagers and a dog.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_fiona_home.response.1": "All right. I wasn’t at home. After the row I couldn’t go home. I had to know what Will would do with Joel’s pages. So I said goodnight and walked to the lift, and when nobody was looking I went back down the floor into the Nest and shut the door. I did the New York call from there.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_fiona_home.response.2": "My Wi-Fi dropped once, for a few minutes after half past nine. That’s all. I never left the room, and I never saw Will. I didn’t know anything had happened until the police rang on Friday.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_fiona_home.title": "Sunshine at nine at night",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_fiona_nest.insight": "Just after half past nine, while Fiona was off the call, the floor’s lights came on bank by bank from the Nest towards Will’s office. Somebody walked out of the Nest to Will’s office, and Fiona was the only person in the Nest.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_fiona_nest.response.0": "…Joel. On his bench. Of course he was.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_fiona_nest.response.1": "Yes. I went to see Will. Gavin had texted me: Will wasn’t signing, the numbers were wrong. So I told the lawyers my Wi-Fi was dying, and I walked down the floor to talk him round.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_fiona_nest.response.2": "We argued. He wouldn’t listen to me. I went back to the Nest and sat there for a few minutes, until I could face three lawyers again, and then I rejoined the call.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_fiona_nest.response.3": "When I left him, Will was alive. I swear it on my children.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_fiona_nest.title": "The lights came on from the Nest",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_gavin_bar.insight": "At 21:06 Gavin stood at No. 31’s street door in the rain, talking to Will on the intercom for thirty-eight seconds. Will didn’t let him in.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_gavin_bar.response.0": "…That’s me. Yes.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_gavin_bar.response.1": "Fiona messaged me at seven: Will was ‘wobbling’. So after dinner I walked over to talk some sense into him. He wouldn’t let me up. He said, through that box, ‘I’m not signing, Gavin. The numbers are wrong. Go back to your hotel.’",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_gavin_bar.response.2": "I texted Fiona and walked back. I was in the bar by twenty past nine; the barman will tell you. I kept quiet because a man who was turned away from the door half an hour before it happened looks exactly like a man who came back.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_gavin_bar.response.3": "And because if Halcyon ever hear the words ‘the numbers are wrong’, there’s no deal in April either. Here. Read them yourself.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_gavin_bar.title": "Gavin was at the door",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_joel_row.insight": "At 17:56 Joel printed ‘Night traffic’ for Will: 412,316 listeners who all listen at exactly three in the morning. The row began at ten past six. It was never about the Bristol office.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_joel_row.response.0": "…You found the other copy. I always print two.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_joel_row.response.1": "I found them in February, chasing a spike on our servers at three in the morning. Four hundred thousand phones that wake up at three, play exactly ten minutes of rain and go back to sleep. Real people don’t do anything at exactly three o’clock.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_joel_row.response.2": "I gave it to Will at six. He said Halcyon’s own people had checked Fiona’s numbers, and I lost my temper. ‘If you sign it tomorrow, I’ll tell them myself.’ Halcyon, I meant. Not the team.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_joel_row.response.3": "On the bench I wrote an email to Halcyon’s lawyers. Four times. I couldn’t send it. Then Will was dead, and the police had me for it. If I’d told them I’d found a fraud and Will had laughed at me and thrown me out, that’s a better motive than anything they had. And if it came out, twenty-two people would lose their jobs. So I told them it was about the office. There’s no plan to close it. I made that up in the interview room.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_joel_row.title": "The row was about the listeners",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_kai_never.insight": "Every one of the 412,316 fake accounts signed up through nightjar.app/r/growthpartners, a link Kai made on 12 September.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_kai_never.response.0": "…Growth Partners. Yeah.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_kai_never.response.1": "In September a contract came down from upstairs, already signed, with a budget I’d never have got past anyone. An agency that ‘seeds engagement’. Every app does it before a sale, I was told, and Halcyon would never look that closely. I made the link, I watched the numbers go up, and I didn’t ask how.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_kai_never.response.2": "They send a report to accounts every month, and I’m copied in. Here’s February’s. It says a director signed the contract, and Nightjar’s got three: Will, Fiona and Gavin. Don’t ask me which. I never wanted to know.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_kai_never.response.3": "I knew they weren’t real people. I just didn’t want to know how not real. But Will didn’t know. I’d swear to that.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_kai_never.title": "Kai made the link",
 "deductions:deductions.d_bench.conclusion": "GPS can’t tell a bench from a door twenty metres away, but a barometer can tell the square from the top of the house. Joel’s watch read nine metres above sea level when he sat down at 21:24, and nine when he stood up at 21:38. Will’s office is fourteen metres above the square: by the stairs or in the lift, the line would have climbed fourteen metres and come back down. His pulse never broke, so the watch never left his wrist, and it counted no steps. Joel sat on the bench for fourteen minutes, and he never went in.",
 "deductions:deductions.d_bench.options.0": "Yes: in by the bike-store door, twenty metres away",
 "deductions:deductions.d_bench.options.1": "Yes: Will came down and let him in at the street door",
 "deductions:deductions.d_bench.options.2": "No: his watch never climbed, and never left his wrist",
 "deductions:deductions.d_bench.options.3": "No: his GPS would have shown him walking to the door",
 "deductions:deductions.d_bench.proofLabels.0": "What his watch recorded on the bench",
 "deductions:deductions.d_bench.proofLabels.1": "How high Will’s office is",
 "deductions:deductions.d_bench.question": "Did Joel go up to Will’s office while he sat on the bench?",
 "deductions:deductions.d_bench.title": "Fourteen minutes on a bench",
 "deductions:deductions.d_found.conclusion": "Since September, 412,316 accounts had played ‘Rain on a Tin Roof’ for exactly ten minutes at exactly three in the morning, and every one of them had signed up through a single link. Halcyon’s £60 million was a price per listener, and without them Nightjar had about 630,000. Joel showed Will at six, and by twenty past nine Will believed him: ‘Joel’s right. I’m not signing it.’ Whoever had bought those listeners had until nine o’clock on Friday morning, when Halcyon expected Will’s signature.",
 "deductions:deductions.d_found.options.0": "That Halcyon meant to close the Bristol office after the sale",
 "deductions:deductions.d_found.options.1": "That four in ten of Nightjar’s listeners were fake",
 "deductions:deductions.d_found.options.2": "That his own share options were worth almost nothing",
 "deductions:deductions.d_found.options.3": "That Larchwood had lied to Halcyon about the price",
 "deductions:deductions.d_found.proofLabels.0": "What Joel found",
 "deductions:deductions.d_found.proofLabels.1": "What Will made of it",
 "deductions:deductions.d_found.question": "What did Joel show Will on Thursday?",
 "deductions:deductions.d_found.title": "The three o’clock listeners",
 "deductions:deductions.d_inside.conclusion": "Nobody went in by either door while Joel watched them, and the intercom let nobody in all evening. At half past nine the lights came on along Nightjar’s floor, bank by bank, from the Nest towards Will’s office. The stairs and the lift come out at Will’s end, so whoever walked to his office walked out of the Nest. Somebody had been in the Nest since 18:34, without a booking.",
 "deductions:deductions.d_inside.options.0": "The bike-store door, with the staff code",
 "deductions:deductions.d_inside.options.1": "The street door, let in by Will",
 "deductions:deductions.d_inside.options.2": "The kitchen, where somebody had waited since six",
 "deductions:deductions.d_inside.options.3": "The Nest, at the far end of Nightjar’s own floor",
 "deductions:deductions.d_inside.proofLabels.0": "What Joel saw at half past nine",
 "deductions:deductions.d_inside.proofLabels.1": "Who was already on the floor",
 "deductions:deductions.d_inside.question": "Where did Will’s killer come from?",
 "deductions:deductions.d_inside.title": "Somebody already upstairs",
 "deductions:deductions.d_light.conclusion": "Will’s office light works from a switch by the door; the sensor lights are only out on the floor. Joel saw it go off at 21:36, two minutes after Will’s heart stopped at 21:34. A dead man doesn’t switch off his light. Somebody was standing in his office after he died, and switched it off on the way out, so the building would look empty. Nobody came in by either door all evening, and the only person on the floor was the one who walked out of the Nest. Fiona says Will was alive when she left him. He wasn’t.",
 "deductions:deductions.d_light.options.0": "Will himself, on his way home",
 "deductions:deductions.d_light.options.1": "The cleaner, early on Friday morning",
 "deductions:deductions.d_light.options.2": "Somebody who was with him after his heart stopped",
 "deductions:deductions.d_light.options.3": "Nobody: the office lights go off by themselves",
 "deductions:deductions.d_light.proofLabels.0": "When Will’s light went off",
 "deductions:deductions.d_light.proofLabels.1": "When Will’s heart stopped",
 "deductions:deductions.d_light.question": "Who switched off Will’s light?",
 "deductions:deductions.d_light.title": "Lights out",
 "deductions:deductions.d_who.conclusion": "At 21:02, and again at 21:51, the sun was shining across Fiona’s kitchen and her clock said ten to three. In Bristol it had been dark since six, and raining all evening. In New York it was four in the afternoon, and nobody on the call thought twice about the sun. The mug in front of her came and went; the light behind her never moved. Fiona’s kitchen was a photograph. She said goodnight at half past six, and at 18:34 the Nest’s sensor saw somebody come in. Joel was on his bench, Kai was on a wall in Bedminster, and Gavin, never let in, was signing for whiskies at his hotel. Fiona took the New York call from the Nest, thirty metres from Will.",
 "deductions:deductions.d_who.options.0": "Joel Lacey",
 "deductions:deductions.d_who.options.1": "Fiona Brodie",
 "deductions:deductions.d_who.options.2": "Gavin Rourke",
 "deductions:deductions.d_who.options.3": "Kai Campbell",
 "deductions:deductions.d_who.proofLabels.0": "Whose alibi doesn’t hold",
 "deductions:deductions.d_who.proofLabels.1": "When the Nest’s visitor arrived",
 "deductions:deductions.d_who.question": "Who was in the Nest on Thursday evening?",
 "deductions:deductions.d_who.title": "In the Nest",
 "dialogues:statements.st_fiona_alive.text": "At half past nine I left the call and went to Will’s office to talk him round. When I went back to the Nest, he was alive.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_fiona_confession.text": "I hit Will with the award, took Joel’s pages, switched off his light and went back to the call.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_fiona_home.text": "At half past six I said goodnight and went home to Clifton. From nine to half past ten I was on the New York call at my kitchen table.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_fiona_nest.text": "I took the New York call from the Nest. The connection dropped for a few minutes after half past nine, but I never left the room, and I never saw Will.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_gavin_bar.text": "After the meeting I went back to my hotel and stayed in the bar until midnight. I never went near Queen Square again.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_gavin_door.text": "At 21:06 Will told me through the intercom that he wasn’t signing, and that the numbers were wrong. I texted Fiona and went back to the bar.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_joel_bench.text": "At 21:24 I sat down on the bench opposite No. 31, and I didn’t get up until 21:38. I never went in.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_joel_found.text": "On Thursday I showed Will that 412,316 of our listeners were fake. On the bench I wrote an email to Halcyon’s lawyers, and never sent it.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_joel_lights.text": "Just after half past nine the lights came on along our floor, bank by bank, from the Nest end towards Will’s office. At 21:36 Will’s light went off. Nobody went in or out of either door while I sat there.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_joel_row.text": "Will and I rowed because Halcyon means to close the Bristol office after the sale, and he hadn’t told the team.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_kai_link.text": "In September I made the link for Growth Partners, an agency paid to supply listeners. I knew they weren’t real.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_kai_never.text": "Every one of our new listeners is a real person. I’ve never heard of fake listeners at Nightjar.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_kai_wall.text": "On Thursday evening I was at the bouldering wall in Bedminster, from about seven until quarter to ten.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_fiona_confirm.lines.0": "Three lawyers in New York, for an hour and a half. The police rang them on Saturday.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_fiona_confirm.lines.1": "They record their calls. They sent me their note and a couple of stills. Here: my kitchen, my tea, my terrible shelves.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_fiona_confirm.prompt": "Can anyone confirm where you were?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_fiona_sale.lines.0": "Halcyon pay by the listener: sixty million pounds for a million of them. Will and I spent seven years on Nightjar. In 2023 I remortgaged my house, twice, to pay the wages. Friday was going to give it back.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_fiona_sale.lines.1": "Halcyon have been very kind. They’ll sign in April instead, for Will. It’s what he’d have wanted.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_fiona_sale.lines.2": "I’d never have believed it of Joel. But he knows the code, and he sat outside that door for a quarter of an hour.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_fiona_sale.prompt": "Tell me about the sale.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_fiona_thursday.lines.0": "Fiona, where were you on Thursday evening?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_fiona_thursday.lines.1": "I said goodnight at half past six and went home to Clifton. From nine until half past ten I was on a video call with Halcyon’s lawyers in New York, at my kitchen table, camera on.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_fiona_thursday.lines.2": "Will should have been on it with me. He said he’d rather read. I thought it was nerves about Friday.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_fiona_thursday.prompt": "Where were you on Thursday evening?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_gavin_confirm.lines.0": "The barman at my hotel, a very patient Portuguese man called Rui. And my bill, since you ask. I sign for everything; my accountant insists.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_gavin_confirm.lines.1": "Here. You’ll see I was drinking steadily, and not in Queen Square.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_gavin_confirm.prompt": "Can anyone confirm where you were?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_gavin_deal.lines.0": "Larchwood put eight million into Nightjar in 2024. Halcyon’s price gives us back twenty-two. My fund needs a win this year, and I’m not ashamed to say so.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_gavin_deal.lines.1": "Will was a sound man who got lucky with a recording of rain. Fiona built the business. Thank God she’s still here.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_gavin_deal.lines.2": "And if you’re asking whether a man in my line of work would kill somebody over a deal: we’d sue them. It’s cheaper.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_gavin_deal.prompt": "What did the sale mean to your fund?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_gavin_thursday.lines.0": "Mr Rourke, where were you on Thursday evening?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_gavin_thursday.lines.1": "With Will and Fiona until twenty past six. Then back to my hotel on the harbour: dinner, and the bar until midnight, with my laptop and a very good Scotch.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_gavin_thursday.lines.2": "I never went near Queen Square again. Why would I? We were signing at nine.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_gavin_thursday.prompt": "Where were you on Thursday evening?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_joel_row.lines.0": "Halcyon. They’re going to close the Bristol office a year after the sale and move engineering to San Francisco. Will knew, and he hadn’t told anyone.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_joel_row.lines.1": "So I said if he signed it tomorrow, I’d tell them myself. The team, I mean.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_joel_row.lines.2": "That’s all it was.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_joel_row.prompt": "What did you and Will row about?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_joel_run.lines.0": "Thursday’s my run home: a loop of the harbour, back through the square, then home to Southville. Ten k, easy. I’m doing London in April.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_joel_run.lines.1": "I came out at ten to nine and warmed up on the steps for five minutes, like always. Then I started the watch and set off at four minutes to.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_joel_run.lines.2": "The app shows moving time, because that’s what runners care about: it stops the clock when you stop. That’s why it says fifty minutes. The police found the other fifteen.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_joel_run.lines.3": "My watch records everything, every second. Here: I’ll download the whole file for you. Read all of it. There’s nothing in it I’m hiding.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_joel_run.prompt": "Tell me about your run.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_joel_thursday.lines.0": "Joel, tell me about Thursday evening.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_joel_thursday.lines.1": "I worked till half past eight. Will was in his office with the door shut. At quarter to nine I changed, knocked on his door and said goodnight. He said, ‘Go home, Joel.’ Not angry. Just tired.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_joel_thursday.lines.2": "I ran my harbour loop, same as every Thursday. When I came back through the square, his light was still on. So I sat down on the bench. Fourteen minutes. His light went off, I sat there a minute or two longer, and then I ran home.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_joel_thursday.lines.3": "I never went in. I told the police. They looked at me like I’d said the dog ate my homework.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_joel_thursday.prompt": "Tell me about Thursday evening.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_joel_view.lines.0": "Joel, what could you see from the bench?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_joel_view.lines.1": "Our floor. The Nest’s light was on; it always is, people forget it. The desks were dark. Will’s light was on behind his blinds. I kept thinking: I’ll go up. I’ll say sorry. I didn’t.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_joel_view.lines.2": "Nobody went in or out of the street door or the bike store. I’d have seen them. I was looking up at those doors every few seconds, working up the nerve.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_joel_view.lines.3": "Then, just after half nine, the lights came on along the floor. Bank by bank, from the Nest end towards Will’s office. Like somebody walking.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_joel_view.lines.4": "At 21:36 Will’s light went off. I know, because I looked at my watch: I’d been sat there twelve minutes. I thought he was coming down at last. For a minute or two I couldn’t decide whether to stay and say sorry, or go before he found me sat there like a lemon. Then I ran.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_joel_view.lines.5": "I never told the police about the lights. They asked me about the bike store, over and over. Only now, telling you, have I thought about which end they started from.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_joel_view.prompt": "What could you see from the bench?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_kai_confirm.lines.0": "Half the wall can. And my mate Dev filmed me falling off the same problem for the fifth week running. He put it on his story, the traitor.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_kai_confirm.lines.1": "Look: that’s me, that’s the café clock, half nine. Dark outside, raining. Thursday.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_kai_confirm.prompt": "Can anyone confirm where you were?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_kai_growth.lines.0": "Kai, how did Nightjar grow so fast last autumn?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_kai_growth.lines.1": "Ads, influencers, a referral scheme, and graft. We went from six hundred thousand monthly listeners to over a million in four months. Best quarter we ever had.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_kai_growth.lines.2": "Fake listeners? No. Never heard of anything like that. Every one of that million is a real person who can’t sleep.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_kai_growth.prompt": "How did Nightjar grow so fast last autumn?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_kai_thursday.lines.0": "I left about twenty to seven and went bouldering in Bedminster, like every Thursday. I was there till quarter to ten.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_kai_thursday.lines.1": "Joel, though… Joel’s the only man in Bristol who’d sit on a bench in the rain for a quarter of an hour because he felt bad about something.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_kai_thursday.prompt": "Where were you on Thursday evening?",
 "evidence:evidence.bench.detail": "A slatted bench on the gravel path along the north side of Queen Square, under a plane tree. Its brass plaque says ‘For Dot, who sat here every day’.\n\nFrom it you look straight up at No. 31: four rows of tall windows, and Nightjar’s are the top row, under the parapet, fourteen metres up. Left to right: the Nest’s single window at the west end, six windows of open-plan desks, and Will’s corner office at the east end, its blinds still down.\n\nTwenty metres to your left, in the side wall, is the bike-store door with its keypad. Thirty metres to your right is the street door and its intercom. From the bench you can see them both.",
 "evidence:evidence.bench.name": "The bench",
 "evidence:evidence.bench.summary": "Joel’s bench faces No. 31. Nightjar’s windows are the top row, fourteen metres up.",
 "evidence:evidence.call_record.detail": "Halcyon’s lawyers in New York sent Fiona their note of Thursday’s call, with two stills from the recording. She forwards them to you.\n\n‘Halcyon / Nightjar, 21:00–22:30 GMT. Present: three of us, and F. Brodie (Nightjar), from home, on camera. Agreed: the papers are final; signing as planned, 09:00 Friday, Bristol.’\n\nStill, 21:02: Fiona at a round wooden table with a mug of tea. Behind her are bookshelves, a wall clock and a tall window. Sunlight falls across the shelves. The clock says ten to three.\n\nStill, 21:51: Fiona at the same table, the mug gone. The same shelves, the same patch of sunlight. The clock says ten to three.",
 "evidence:evidence.call_record.name": "The New York call",
 "evidence:evidence.call_record.summary": "The lawyers’ note of the call, with two stills: Fiona on camera at her kitchen table from nine until half past ten.",
 "evidence:evidence.gavin_bill.detail": "Gavin’s bill from his hotel on the harbour, ten minutes’ walk from Queen Square: Thursday 5 March, charged to room 214, each line signed ‘G. Rourke’ at the bar.\n\n19:12 · Dinner for one\n20:38 · Talisker 18\n21:22 · Talisker 18\n21:36 · Talisker 18\n21:51 · Talisker 18\n22:40 · Talisker 18\n23:52 · Sparkling water",
 "evidence:evidence.gavin_bill.name": "Gavin’s bar bill",
 "evidence:evidence.gavin_bill.summary": "Thursday’s bill from Gavin’s hotel bar: a whisky signed for at 20:38, then 21:22, 21:36 and 21:51.",
 "evidence:evidence.gavin_messages.detail": "Gavin’s phone: his messages with Fiona on Thursday.\n\n19:00 Fiona: He’s wobbling. Joel’s got at him\n21:09 Gavin: Will won’t let me up. Says he’s not signing. Says the numbers are ‘wrong’. CALL ME\n21:12 Fiona: On with NY. Leave it with me.\n22:34 Gavin: Well??\n22:58 Fiona: Go to bed Gavin",
 "evidence:evidence.gavin_messages.name": "Gavin’s messages",
 "evidence:evidence.gavin_messages.summary": "At 21:09 Gavin told Fiona that Will wasn’t signing. At 21:12 she answered: ‘Leave it with me.’",
 "evidence:evidence.growth_report.detail": "An email Kai forwards you, sent on 2 March by Growth Partners Ltd. He is only copied in.\n\n‘To: Accounts, Nightjar · Cc: Kai Campbell\nFebruary: 412,316 engaged listeners maintained (target met).\nInvoice GP-0226: £41,200, payable under your agreement of 11 September, signed for Nightjar by a director.\nAs you asked, all activity is scheduled for 03:00, outside UK daytime.’",
 "evidence:evidence.growth_report.name": "Growth Partners’ report",
 "evidence:evidence.growth_report.summary": "February’s report from the agency that supplied the listeners: 412,316 of them, under an agreement a director signed.",
 "evidence:evidence.intercom_log.detail": "The intercom screen in the hall keeps a week of calls, each with a still from the camera in the street door.\n\nThursday 5 March:\n16:28 — to Nightjar. Answered. Door released. A tall man in a camel coat, wheeling a small suitcase.\n21:06 — to Nightjar. Answered on the office handset, 38 seconds. Door not released. The same tall man in the camel coat, collar up and rain on his shoulders, looking up into the camera.\n\nNo other calls after 18:00. No door released after 16:28.",
 "evidence:evidence.intercom_log.name": "The intercom",
 "evidence:evidence.intercom_log.summary": "One call to Nightjar after six on Thursday: a man in a camel coat at 21:06. The door stayed shut.",
 "evidence:evidence.joel_run.detail": "Joel’s run on his running app, as the police printed it.\n\n‘Thursday run home’ · Thursday 5 March 2026 · 20:56–22:01 · 7°C, light rain\nDistance 10.4 km · Moving time 49:58 · Elapsed time 1:05:20 · Pace 4:48 /km\n\n(Moving time stops the clock whenever you stop. Elapsed time never stops.)\n\nMap: from No. 31 Queen Square, a loop of the harbour: Prince Street Bridge, Wapping Wharf, the Cumberland Basin and back along Hotwells. Into Queen Square again at 21:24, then south over the river and round Victoria Park, home to Southville.\n\nPace graph: flat from 21:24 to 21:38, at a bench on the north side of Queen Square, opposite No. 31.",
 "evidence:evidence.joel_run.name": "Joel’s run",
 "evidence:evidence.joel_run.summary": "10.4 km in fifty minutes of moving time, and sixty-five in all: fourteen of them on a bench in Queen Square.",
 "evidence:evidence.kai_video.detail": "A video in Kai’s story, posted at 21:31 on Thursday by his climbing partner. Kai is on the overhang at the bouldering wall in Bedminster. He reaches for the last hold, misses, and drops onto the mat. Behind him, the clock above the café counter says 21:30, and through the café’s windows the street is dark and wet.\n\nThe caption: ‘week five. still no 😤’",
 "evidence:evidence.kai_video.name": "Kai’s video",
 "evidence:evidence.kai_video.summary": "Posted at 21:31: Kai on the bouldering wall in Bedminster, falling off the last hold.",
 "evidence:evidence.nest_panel.detail": "The booking screen beside the Nest’s door, open at Thursday:\n\n14:00–15:00 · Booked · Design review\n18:34–22:52 · In use · Not booked (sensor)\n\nUnderneath, in small grey letters: ‘Please book the rooms you use! Unbooked use is reported to the office manager. A room stays in use until the sensor has seen nobody for fifteen minutes.’\n\nThe Nest is a room for one or two at the west end of the floor, as far from the stairs and the lift as you can get. It has a frosted-glass door, a small round wooden table, a screen and a window onto the square.",
 "evidence:evidence.nest_panel.name": "The Nest’s screen",
 "evidence:evidence.nest_panel.summary": "Nobody booked the Nest on Thursday evening. Its sensor saw somebody there from 18:34 to 22:52.",
 "evidence:evidence.police_summary.detail": "Avon and Somerset Police — summary for the family. Thursday 5 March 2026, a wet night.\n\nMr W. HARDING (43), chief executive of Nightjar Ltd, was found dead at 07:10 on Friday by the cleaner, at his desk in his corner office. Nightjar’s floor is the top floor of No. 31 Queen Square, fourteen metres above the square. Cause of death: blows to the head with a glass award from his office shelf, found on the floor, wiped. His office light, which works from a switch by the door, was off, and his blinds were down.\n\nTime of death: Mr HARDING’s sports watch recorded his pulse every second. It rose to 148 at 21:33 and stopped at 21:34.\n\nAccess after 18:00: the street door, by staff badge or the intercom (no badge entries; no door released); or the bike-store door on the square, by a keypad whose code all staff know (not logged). All other staff had left by 18:45, and have accounted for their evening.\n\nMr J. LACEY (29), lead developer. At 18:10, in the glass meeting room, staff heard him shout at Mr HARDING: ‘If you sign it tomorrow, I’ll tell them myself!’ He left at 20:51 and ran home. From 21:24 to 21:38 his running app places him on a bench in Queen Square, twenty metres from the bike-store door. A watch’s GPS is good to about ten metres beside tall buildings. We believe he went in by the bike store during those fourteen minutes.\n\nArrested Saturday 7 March, and released on bail. The Crown Prosecution Service is considering a charge of murder.",
 "evidence:evidence.police_summary.name": "Police summary",
 "evidence:evidence.police_summary.summary": "Will died at 21:34. The police say Joel went in by the bike store while he sat outside.",
 "evidence:evidence.printout.detail": "Three pages from the printer tray, under a stack of Halcyon’s contract drafts, printed from Joel’s laptop at 17:56 on Thursday: two copies, and this is the second. At the top: ‘NIGHT TRAFFIC — for Will’.\n\nPage 1 is a graph of Nightjar’s listening, minute by minute, every night since September: low and ragged all night long, and a needle at exactly 03:00.\n\nPage 2: ‘412,316 accounts. Every one plays “Rain on a Tin Roof” for exactly 10:00, starting at 03:00:00, every night since 14 September. Nine phone models. One app version. Not one has ever paused, skipped or rated a track. All signed up through one link: nightjar.app/r/growthpartners, made by Kai on 12 September.’\n\nPage 3: ‘Halcyon pay per monthly listener: £60m for 1.04 million. Without these accounts we have about 630,000. Real people don’t do anything at exactly three o’clock. Will, please read this before Friday. J.’",
 "evidence:evidence.printout.name": "Night traffic",
 "evidence:evidence.printout.summary": "Three pages Joel printed at 17:56 on Thursday: 412,316 listeners who all listen at exactly three in the morning.",
 "evidence:evidence.run_export.detail": "The file Joel’s watch recorded on Thursday, a line for every second. Joel downloads it for you from his account. On the bench, from 21:24:12 to 21:38:02:\n\nPosition: the bench, give or take nine metres.\nElevation, from the watch’s barometer: 9 metres above sea level when he sat down, and 9 metres when he stood up. Never above 10 in between.\nHeart rate: 126 as he sat down, settling to 64–71 by 21:27, unbroken to the end. (The watch reads the pulse through the skin of the wrist. Take it off, and the line stops.)\nSteps: none.\n\nAt 21:38:02 he is running again, south out of the square.",
 "evidence:evidence.run_export.name": "Joel’s watch file",
 "evidence:evidence.run_export.summary": "Every second Joel’s watch recorded on the bench: where he was, how high, and his pulse.",
 "evidence:evidence.wills_messages.detail": "Claire’s phone: her messages with Will on Thursday 5 March.\n\n17:02 Will: Big night. Don’t wait up x\n19:48 Claire: Kids are down. Have you eaten?\n20:05 Will: Pizza at my desk. Reading something Joel gave me\n21:20 Will: Joel’s right. I’m not signing it. I’ll tell you everything tomorrow x\n22:31 Claire: Coming home?\n23:40 Claire: Will?\n01:12 Claire: Please answer me",
 "evidence:evidence.wills_messages.name": "Will’s messages",
 "evidence:evidence.wills_messages.summary": "Will and Claire on Thursday, ending at 21:20: ‘Joel’s right. I’m not signing it.’",
 "suspects:suspects.fiona.bio": "Will’s co-founder and Nightjar’s chief operating officer, the one who kept the lights on. In 2023 she remortgaged her house in Clifton, twice, to pay the wages. Since Friday she has been running Nightjar, and holding the Halcyon deal together.",
 "suspects:suspects.fiona.dismissal": "I’m sorry, I don’t see what you’re asking.",
 "suspects:suspects.fiona.name": "Fiona Brodie",
 "suspects:suspects.fiona.rebuttal": "Will was my oldest friend. I spent that evening in front of three lawyers. You’ve got this wrong.",
 "suspects:suspects.fiona.role": "The co-founder",
 "suspects:suspects.gavin.bio": "A partner at Larchwood Ventures in London, which put £8 million into Nightjar in 2024 and has a seat on its board. He came to Bristol on Thursday for Friday’s signing, and he’s still at his hotel on the harbour.",
 "suspects:suspects.gavin.dismissal": "I fail to see the relevance.",
 "suspects:suspects.gavin.name": "Gavin Rourke",
 "suspects:suspects.gavin.rebuttal": "I lost a friend and eight million pounds on Thursday night. I’d choose your next words very carefully.",
 "suspects:suspects.gavin.role": "The investor",
 "suspects:suspects.joel.bio": "Nightjar’s lead developer and its second employee: he joined at twenty-two, straight from university, and wrote the engine that plays the rain. He runs every day in all weathers, and he’s running London in April. On Thursdays he runs home from the office.",
 "suspects:suspects.joel.dismissal": "Sorry. I don’t follow. Could you put that another way?",
 "suspects:suspects.joel.name": "Joel Lacey",
 "suspects:suspects.joel.rebuttal": "I sat on a bench. I wanted to go up and say sorry to him, and I couldn’t make myself do it. That’s the worst thing I did on Thursday.",
 "suspects:suspects.joel.role": "The developer",
 "suspects:suspects.kai.bio": "Nightjar’s head of growth, Bristol born and bred: ads, influencers and referral links. Since September he has taken Nightjar from six hundred thousand monthly listeners to over a million. On Thursdays he goes bouldering.",
 "suspects:suspects.kai.dismissal": "Not with you, sorry.",
 "suspects:suspects.kai.name": "Kai Campbell",
 "suspects:suspects.kai.rebuttal": "I was halfway up a wall in Bedminster. Ask anyone who was there.",
 "suspects:suspects.kai.role": "The head of growth"
}
